#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442814} :activity/id #uuid "4001c9a4-1021-5a1b-9693-811b63a41207" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050130 :topic/id #uuid "2f232786-4262-5d64-9c58-1578e8c629d2" :topic/math-academy-id 2129} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c73f4b81-f7c2-58b3-ba59-81c1cb4e21d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395102} {:answer/id #uuid "2965e000-8ed9-5b43-8807-b337179e3e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395103} {:answer/id #uuid "f728b525-0316-5b5a-bee0-0f67b27c8544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395104} {:answer/id #uuid "5578869c-e22f-59c6-b9c4-7f6a4a040091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395105} {:answer/id #uuid "db6dd2c4-cdf3-588c-b826-6965e60d9428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395105} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8d465ac-6125-5625-8e53-c378575651c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a11a1ea5-30ff-5154-92c7-cf0778166ca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96026" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} & \\frac{2}{7} & \\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{6}{7} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot 0=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "24c95cd3-c302-51a3-abac-08bfbbb757dc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5efe4aae-2768-528f-a3cc-4c9b9a907391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379830} {:answer/id #uuid "a5705d08-f2bc-5a4f-b1ab-65641a5b0ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379831} {:answer/id #uuid "bf62a0e5-63e3-5e42-889b-986f2738de40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379832} {:answer/id #uuid "e87b5762-481c-57bd-ba1b-c18fef4d4c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379833} {:answer/id #uuid "411c482c-f2e8-5cb9-b82c-84752b240dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379831} :answer-field/id #uuid "a20ad309-608a-5c10-aca7-0636d5c13dac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9fc92fc5-cd9a-5882-b3a5-3c9b7ba00cb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-41417" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=4,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=2,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=6,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-2{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-4{v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{4}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{6}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-2{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-4{v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}={v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=8{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=4{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=16{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+16+6=26.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2813bf88-52e1-5e7e-aeff-5a25b21a24b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442813}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c232a99-6645-5177-89dc-66f13905956a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415488} {:answer/id #uuid "713fedd8-bf09-5849-a1cc-c36eb8963d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415491} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2e1681-8914-5558-96cf-ff55332d7ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415494} {:answer/id #uuid "bdaadcc3-b0d6-5daa-b301-47336aa1a8b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415496} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb4da72-b7ae-5fa0-aa7f-27b95ecda700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415497} :answer-field/id #uuid "84577b17-c582-5baa-9501-36a73798f439" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a03feca5-d649-5b96-991d-3e2972b12233" :question/math-academy-id "q-95937" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{12}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-2{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=4{q}_{1}-8{q}_{2}+4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{12}+{r}_{33}=3+(-2)+4=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "668df193-99f3-547e-8195-6dfa92ee8c3d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442811}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3529dcc2-d908-58eb-8e99-7d1d828d18b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442804} {:answer/id #uuid "387b9ac5-2487-5c7a-a0c7-85adba10d944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442805} {:answer/id #uuid "af55c525-fcb4-5409-9a04-32dc1d2a0e2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442806} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0166a7-27f9-57f1-b2b0-ed7ed1491514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442807} {:answer/id #uuid "c403d147-73fe-5fb4-8186-c08fd069f494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442807} :answer-field/id #uuid "b927eac4-98a6-5bd4-97b1-9da46ea6a1a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "71405c00-ca6f-5d4e-8940-325d2e48014e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40841" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{3})=\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "73796bfe-825e-5f2c-aa11-3af559c65185" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e36d9c-e9a2-5446-ad66-2c509425fb2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364622} {:answer/id #uuid "6838046c-6bb6-5382-aff4-79052bb83389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364623} {:answer/id #uuid "66729efb-cb03-5e0d-975f-24a9597e8440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364624} {:answer/id #uuid "0e78a432-6e13-5220-8bcd-195f0ec965d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364625} {:answer/id #uuid "eac12345-022f-5cbe-aa4a-767d1ed83136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364624} :answer-field/id #uuid "736f5b90-2f10-5a15-8216-cf8f1586fc1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5c7952d1-1f1c-50a2-a342-5271912d43de" :question/math-academy-id "q-96033" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{6}{18})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (3\\sqrt{2})=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "aaefce95-2575-5d4e-9758-6032bf02fe49"}] :activity/title "QR Factorization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442803}]}
